/* crosswalk cards, r10. */
(window.LADDER_CARDS=window.LADDER_CARDS||{})["10a"]=[{"q":"lim_{x→3} f(x) = 5. What is 5 called?","a":"a limit of f at 3","w":["the value f(3)","the reach of f at 3","the gap of f at 3"],"why":"The line says f heads to 5 at 3; it says nothing about f(3)."},
 {"q":"{x ∈ ℝ : 0 < |x − 2| < 0.1}. What is this set called?","a":"the neighbourhood of 2 of reach 0.1","w":["the interval (1.9, 2.1)","the target of 2 of gap 0.1","the domain of f near 2"],"why":"The 0 < takes 2 itself out; (1.9, 2.1) keeps it."},
 {"q":"H(x) = (x² − 1)/(x − 1) on ℝ ∖ {1}. What does H have at 1?","a":"a hole: defined near 1, no value at 1","w":["no limit at 1","the value 2 at 1","a value but no limit at 1"],"why":"A hole is defined near a with a not an input; H agrees with x + 1 near 1."},
 {"q":"H(x) = (x² − 1)/(x − 1) on ℝ ∖ {1}. Is lim_{x→1} H(x) = 2 so?","a":"yes, by agreeing-near with x + 1","w":["no: H has no value at 1","no: the bottom is 0 at 1","yes, since H(1) = 2"],"why":"Near 1, H(x) = x + 1, and x + 1 heads to 2 at 1."},
 {"q":"A function equals x + 1 except a stray value 3 at 1. What is its limit at 1?","a":"2, the stray value plays no part","w":["3, the value at 1","none: the value breaks it","both 2 and 3"],"why":"The 0 < keeps the input 1 out of the game."},
 {"q":"x: 1.9, 1.99, 2.01, 2.1 gives f(x): 6.7, 6.97, 7.03, 7.3. What does the table do?","a":"suggests a limit of 7 at 2","w":["proves the limit at 2 is 7","shows f(2) = 7","proves f has no hole at 2"],"why":"Four rounds are not a proof; the line needs a δ for every ε."},
 {"q":"Against ε, a player answers δ = ε/3 for x ↦ 3x + 1 at 2. Does it win?","a":"yes: |3x + 1 − 7| = 3|x − 2| < ε","w":["no: δ must not use ε","no: it needs δ = 3ε","only when ε < 1"],"why":"Line-limit: δ = ε/|m| with m = 3."},
 {"q":"A player answers δ = ε/(2x) for x ↦ x² at 3. What is wrong?","a":"δ has x in it","w":["δ has ε in it","δ is not positive","nothing: it wins"],"why":"δ is chosen before x; it may hang on ε and w only."},
 {"q":"∃δ > 0 ∀ε > 0 : … in place of ∀ε > 0 ∃δ > 0 : …. What changes?","a":"one reach must beat every gap","w":["nothing: the order is free","the gap may now hang on δ","the line gets weaker"],"why":"∃∀ is the stronger claim; it makes f exactly L near w."},
 {"q":"x² at w: first promise |x − w| < 1, then δ = min(1, ε/(1 + 2|w|)). Which technique?","a":"square-limit","w":["line-limit","one-over-x-limit","agreeing-near"],"why":"The first promise bounds |x + w| by 1 + 2|w|."},
 {"q":"1/x at w ≠ 0: first promise |x − w| < |w|/2. What does it keep off?","a":"inputs near 0","w":["inputs near w","inputs above w","gaps above 1"],"why":"One-over-x-limit keeps x away from 0 so 1/|x| stays below 2/|w|."},
 {"q":"A line reads lim_{x→1} H(x) = H(1). Is this a move of the page?","a":"no: H may have no value at 1","w":["yes: put in the input","yes: by agreeing-near","yes: limits are values"],"why":"The limit sees only a neighbourhood with 1 removed."}];
(window.LADDER_CARDS=window.LADDER_CARDS||{})["10b"]=[{"q":"lim_{x→w} f(x) = L and lim_{x→w} f(x) = M, f defined near w. What follows?","a":"L = M, by one-limit","w":["L ≤ M only","nothing: the two may differ","L ≠ M, by two-targets"],"why":"One-limit: a function cannot head two ways at one input."},
 {"q":"Two limits L and M are challenged with ε = |L − M|/2. Why that ε?","a":"the two targets share no real","w":["it is the largest ε that overlaps","it makes δ the same for both","it keeps f defined near w"],"why":"Two-targets: half-widths add, so 2ε ≤ |L − M| keeps them apart."},
 {"q":"L = 1, M = 4, ε = 3. Is some real within 3 of both?","a":"yes: 2.5","w":["no: the targets are apart","no: only 1 and 4 are","yes, but only 4"],"why":"ε = |L − M| makes the targets overlap; take half or less."},
 {"q":"The one-limit column takes δ = min(min(δ₁, δ₂), r). Why the least?","a":"the input must sit in every window","w":["the largest window wins more","δ₁ alone is enough","min makes ε smaller"],"why":"Each promise holds only in its own window; lesser-below-both."},
 {"q":"A proof picks x = w to clash the two promises. What is wrong?","a":"w is not in any neighbourhood of w","w":["w is too far from w","x must be a whole number","nothing: x = w works"],"why":"The neighbourhood leaves w out; the column uses w + δ/2."},
 {"q":"“The limit is unique, so it exists.” Is this a move of the page?","a":"no: one-limit is only the at-most-one","w":["yes: ∃! is one-limit","yes, for f defined near w","no: unique limits never exist"],"why":"∃! is a some and an at-most-one; 1/x at 0 has no limit."},
 {"q":"f(x) ≤ g(x) near w, f → L and g → M at w. What follows?","a":"L ≤ M","w":["L < M","L = M","nothing about L and M"],"why":"Limits-keep-order passes ≤ from the outputs to the limits."},
 {"q":"−|x| < |x| for every x ≠ 0. What are the limits at 0?","a":"both 0","w":["−0 below 0","none: strict order fails","0 and a value below 0"],"why":"Limits-keep-order keeps only ≤, never <."},
 {"q":"lim_{x→w} f(x) = 6. What does sign-kept-near give?","a":"f(x) > 3 near w","w":["f(x) > 6 near w","f(w) > 0","f(x) = 6 near w"],"why":"Challenge with ε = L/2: near w, f(x) > L − L/2."},
 {"q":"lim_{x→0} 1/x = lim_{x→0} 1/x, by the self-sameness axiom. Is this so?","a":"no: the name names nothing here","w":["yes: every name is itself","yes, by one-limit","no: 1/x is not defined"],"why":"1/x has no limit at 0, so the name has no thing to name."},
 {"q":"3x + 1 heads to 7 at 2. What does one-limit give for 7.5?","a":"3x + 1 does not head to 7.5 at 2","w":["3x + 1 heads to 7.5 too","nothing until it is checked","7.5 is the value at 2"],"why":"If it did, one-limit would make 7 = 7.5."},
 {"q":"The one-limit column refutes L ≠ M. Which move turns ¬¬(L = M) into L = M?","a":"not-not-out","w":["refute","two-targets","lesser-below-both"],"why":"Refute gives ¬(L ≠ M); not-not-out, from rung 7, drops the two nots."}];
(window.LADDER_CARDS=window.LADDER_CARDS||{})["10c"]=[{"q":"lim_{x→2} f(x) = 3 and lim_{x→2} g(x) = −2. What is lim_{x→2} (fg)(x)?","a":"−6, by limits-multiply","w":["1, by limits-add","−3/2, by limits-divide","none: g heads below 0"],"why":"Limits-multiply: fg heads to LM."},
 {"q":"lim_{x→4} f(x) = 5 and lim_{x→4} g(x) = 0. What does limits-divide give for f/g at 4?","a":"nothing: the bottom heads to 0","w":["it heads to 0","it heads to 5","it has no limit"],"why":"Limits-divide needs M ≠ 0; with M = 0 it is silent."},
 {"q":"lim_{x→1} f(x) = 2. What is lim_{x→1} (7f)(x)?","a":"14, by constant-factor","w":["9, by limits-add","2, by agreeing-near","7, by line-limit"],"why":"Constant-factor: cf heads to cL."},
 {"q":"lim_{x→3} (x² − 2x + 4). Which technique gives 7 in one line?","a":"substitution","w":["agreeing-near","one-limit","away-from-zero"],"why":"For a polynomial p, the limit at w is p(w)."},
 {"q":"lim_{x→2} (x + 3)/(x − 1). May you put in x = 2?","a":"yes: the bottom at 2 is 1, not 0","w":["no: it is a quotient","no: 2 is not an input","yes, at every input"],"why":"Substitution’s second half needs q(w) ≠ 0."},
 {"q":"A proof of limits-add gives each of f and g the gap ε/2. What is ε/2 called?","a":"a budget","w":["a reach","a first promise","a neighbourhood"],"why":"Each piece gets half; the triangle adds the halves to ε."},
 {"q":"f(x)g(x) − LM = f(x)(g(x) − M) + M(f(x) − L). What move made it?","a":"add and take away f(x)M","w":["the triangle inequality","spread on both factors","cancel f(x) from both sides"],"why":"The middle pair is 0, so only the look changes."},
 {"q":"In limits-multiply, the first reach makes |f(x)| < |L| + 1. Which technique?","a":"bounded-near","w":["away-from-zero","sign-kept-near","lesser-below-both"],"why":"Challenge with ε = 1; the reverse triangle gives the bound."},
 {"q":"lim g = M ≠ 0. Near w, |g(x)| > |M|/2. Which technique?","a":"away-from-zero","w":["bounded-near","reciprocal-limit","limits-keep-order"],"why":"Challenge with ε = |M|/2, then the reverse triangle."},
 {"q":"A budget reads ε/(2|M|) with M = 0. What is wrong?","a":"it names nothing: dividing by 0","w":["it is too large","it is not positive yet","nothing: M = 0 is fine"],"why":"The page uses |M| + 1, which is never 0."},
 {"q":"|x|/x + (−|x|/x) heads to 0 at 0. Do |x|/x and −|x|/x have limits at 0?","a":"no: neither has one","w":["yes: both head to 0","yes: they head to 1 and −1","yes, by limits-add backwards"],"why":"Limits-add runs one way: a limit of the sum says nothing of the parts."},
 {"q":"Limits-add, limits-multiply, limits-divide and their kin. What is their accepted name?","a":"the limit laws","w":["the order laws","the reach laws","the budget techniques"],"why":"The techniques for limits of sums, products and quotients."}];
(window.LADDER_CARDS=window.LADDER_CARDS||{})["10d"]=[{"q":"4 − x² ≤ f(x) ≤ 4 + x² for every x near 0. What is lim_{x→0} f(x)?","a":"4, by the squeeze","w":["0, by squeezed-to-0","none: f has no formula","f(0), by substitution"],"why":"Both traps head to 4 at 0, and f sits between them."},
 {"q":"0 ≤ f(x) ≤ 1 for every x near 2. Does the squeeze give a limit of f at 2?","a":"no: the traps head to 0 and 1","w":["yes: the limit is ½","yes: the limit is 0","yes: the limit is 1"],"why":"The traps must head to one value; q has no limit and sits there."},
 {"q":"|f(x)| ≤ |x − 3| near 3. What is lim_{x→3} f(x)?","a":"0, by squeezed-to-0","w":["3, by squeezed-to-0","none: one side only","f(3), by the squeeze"],"why":"h(x) = |x − 3| heads to 0 at 3, and |f| sits below it."},
 {"q":"|f(x) − 6| ≤ 2|x − 1| near 1. Which technique gives lim_{x→1} f(x) = 6?","a":"squeezed-by-distance","w":["limits-keep-order","away-from-zero","agreeing-near"],"why":"A bound C·|x − w| on the distance from L, with C = 2."},
 {"q":"q is 1 at every fraction and 0 at every irrational. Does q have a limit at 0?","a":"no: heads-nowhere","w":["yes: 0","yes: ½","yes: 1, as 0 is a fraction"],"why":"Every window holds a fraction and an irrational; ε = ½ beats every L."},
 {"q":"q as above. What is lim_{x→0} x · q(x)?","a":"0, by squeezed-to-0","w":["none, by heads-nowhere","1, as q(0) = 1","0 · q(0), by substitution"],"why":"|x · q(x)| ≤ |x|, and |x| heads to 0 at 0."},
 {"q":"x · q(x) heads to 0 at 0. Does q head to 0 at 0?","a":"no: the factor x did the work","w":["yes: drop the factor x","yes, by limits-divide","yes: q(x) = 0 at irrationals"],"why":"Without the x, heads-nowhere says q has no limit."},
 {"q":"r is a fraction and n ≠ 0 a number. Is r + √2/n a fraction?","a":"no: it is irrational","w":["yes: a sum of fractions","yes, when n is large","only when r = 0"],"why":"A fraction plus an irrational is irrational (9a)."},
 {"q":"q(x) ≥ 0 near 0, and the constant 0 heads to 0. Is q squeezed to 0?","a":"no: one trap is not a squeeze","w":["yes, from below","yes: 0 is the least output","yes, at fractions"],"why":"The squeeze needs a trap on both sides that heads to one value."},
 {"q":"|f(x) − 2| ≤ 3|x − 1| checked at x = 1.1, 1.01 and 1.001. Is the bound proved?","a":"no: it must hold on a neighbourhood","w":["yes: three inputs suffice","yes, by squeezed-by-distance","no: it needs a fourth input"],"why":"The hypothesis is for every x near w; a few inputs prove nothing."},
 {"q":"|√(x + 4) − 2| ≤ ½|x| on a reach of 4. What δ wins against ε?","a":"min(4, 2ε)","w":["min(4, ε/2)","2ε alone","min(½, ε)"],"why":"A bound C·|x − w| on reach r gives δ = min(r, ε/C)."},
 {"q":"(x − 2)² · q(x) + 5 near 2. What does it head to at 2?","a":"5","w":["none: q is wild","5 + q(2)","0"],"why":"|f(x) − 5| ≤ (x − 2)², which heads to 0 at 2."}];
(window.LADDER_CARDS=window.LADDER_CARDS||{})["10e"]=[{"q":"f(x) = x + 2 for x < 1, f(x) = 4x − 1 for x > 1. Does f have a limit at 1?","a":"yes: 3, both sides agree","w":["no: two formulas meet at 1","no: f has no value at 1","yes: 3 from the left only"],"why":"From the left x + 2 heads to 3; from the right 4x − 1 heads to 3."},
 {"q":"g(x) = x² for x < 2, g(x) = x + 1 for x > 2. What does g have at 2?","a":"a jump: 4 on the left, 3 on the right","w":["the limit 4","the limit 3","the limit 3.5"],"why":"Two-sides-differ: 4 ≠ 3, so no limit at 2."},
 {"q":"|x|/x at 0. What are its limits from the left and from the right?","a":"−1 from the left, 1 from the right","w":["1 from the left, −1 from the right","0 from both sides","1 from both sides"],"why":"|x| = −x for x < 0 and x for x > 0."},
 {"q":"lim_{x→0} √x = 0. Is the line so?","a":"no: √x is not defined near 0","w":["yes, by root-at-0","yes, by substitution","no: the limit is 1"],"why":"No input is left of 0; only the right-hand line applies."},
 {"q":"lim_{x→0⁺} √x = 0 against ε. What δ does root-at-0 answer?","a":"ε²","w":["ε/2","√ε","2ε"],"why":"For 0 < x < ε², √x < ε."},
 {"q":"In x → 3⁺, what is the small high +?","a":"part of the mark: inputs from the right","w":["the next real after 3","a power of 3","the sign of a positive 3"],"why":"3⁺ names no real; there is no next real after 3."},
 {"q":"f heads to 5 from the left of 2 and to 5 from the right, and f(2) = 0. What is lim_{x→2} f(x)?","a":"5","w":["0","none: the value breaks it","5 from one side only"],"why":"Two-sides-agree; the value at 2 is not consulted."},
 {"q":"1/x near 0. Does it have a limit from the right?","a":"no: no-side-at-0","w":["yes: 0","yes: a large real","only from the left"],"why":"Against ε = 1, an input min(δ/2, 1/(|L| + 2)) beats every L."},
 {"q":"One-sided proof answers δ = max(δ⁻, δ⁺). What is wrong?","a":"the larger reach breaks a side’s promise","w":["max is not defined on reals","δ must be ε/2","nothing: it is the least"],"why":"Two-sides-agree takes the least of the two reaches."},
 {"q":"(x² + x)/|x| at 0. What happens?","a":"1 from the right, −1 from the left","w":["the limit is 1","the limit is 0","the limit is −1"],"why":"Cancel x on each side with the formula that side uses."},
 {"q":"A function wins on the right with 1 at 0. Does it have a limit at 0?","a":"not yet: the left must agree","w":["yes: 1","yes: one side is enough","no: one-sided limits never do"],"why":"Two-sides-agree needs both sides with one value."},
 {"q":"Limits-add used with x → w⁺ in place of x → w. What licenses it?","a":"sides-too","w":["two-sides-agree","one-side-one-value","agreeing-near"],"why":"Each column’s window becomes a half-window, and nothing else changes."}];
(window.LADDER_CARDS=window.LADDER_CARDS||{})["10f"]=[{"q":"lim_{x→3} (x² − 9)/(x − 3). Substituting gives 0/0. What is that?","a":"a signal: x − 3 hides in both","w":["the answer 1","the answer 0","proof of no limit"],"why":"Top and bottom are 0 at 3, so each has the factor x − 3."},
 {"q":"lim_{x→3} (x² − 9)/(x − 3) = lim_{x→3} (x + 3). What licenses the cancel?","a":"agreeing-near","w":["substitution","limits-divide","one-limit"],"why":"The two agree at every x ≠ 3, and a limit needs no more."},
 {"q":"lim_{x→2} (x + 1)/(x − 2). The top is 3 and the bottom 0 at 2. What follows?","a":"no limit: outputs pass a bound","w":["a signal to factor and cancel","the limit is 0","the limit is 3"],"why":"A top not 0 over a bottom heading to 0; check each side."},
 {"q":"(√(x + 9) − 3)/x at 0, both 0. What is the move?","a":"times the conjugate over itself","w":["factor the top and cancel","divide by the greatest power","split by sides"],"why":"The conjugate √(x + 9) + 3 clears the root by difference-of-squares."},
 {"q":"lim_{x→∞} (4x² − x)/(2x² + 7). What does it head to?","a":"2","w":["0","4","no limit"],"why":"Equal degrees: the leading coefficients give 4/2."},
 {"q":"lim_{x→∞} (3x + 5)/(x² + 1). What does it head to?","a":"0","w":["3","5","no limit"],"why":"The top’s degree is less: rational-functions-far-out."},
 {"q":"f(x) = (2x − 1)/(x + 4) heads to 2 far out. What is y = 2 called?","a":"a horizontal asymptote of f","w":["a vertical asymptote of f","a threshold of f","the value f(2)"],"why":"A limit far out gives the horizontal asymptote y = L."},
 {"q":"1/(x − 5) passes every bound as x → 5⁺. What is x = 5 called?","a":"a vertical asymptote","w":["a horizontal asymptote","a hole","a threshold"],"why":"Outputs pass every bound as x heads to 5 from a side."},
 {"q":"lim_{x→0⁺} 1/x = ∞. Is this a limit in 10a’s sense?","a":"no: it is shorthand for passing every bound","w":["yes: the limit is ∞","yes: from the right only","no: 1/x is not defined there"],"why":"∞ is not a number; passing-every-bound refutes every L."},
 {"q":"√(x² + x) − x far out. “Big minus big is 0.” Is that so?","a":"no: it heads to ½","w":["yes: both pass every bound","yes, by limits-subtract","no: it passes every bound"],"why":"Rationalise: x/(√(x² + x) + x) heads to ½."},
 {"q":"(x² + 4)/(x + 2): cancel the x’s to get (x + 4)/2. What is wrong?","a":"cancel factors, not terms","w":["nothing: it is legal","cancel only the 4 and 2","the bottom heads to 0"],"why":"At −2 the top is 8, so x + 2 is not a factor."},
 {"q":"a_n = 1/S(n): 1, ½, ⅓, …. What does the sequence do?","a":"converges to 0","w":["converges to 1","passes below every bound","stands still past some N"],"why":"One-over-n: every ε has a threshold N past which 1/S(n) < ε."}];
(window.LADDER_CARDS=window.LADDER_CARDS||{})["10g"]=[{"q":"Prove lim_{x→2} (4x − 3) = 5. What δ answers ε?","a":"ε/4","w":["4ε","ε/5","ε/3"],"why":"|4x − 3 − 5| = 4|x − 2|: the leftover is the constant 4."},
 {"q":"|x² + 3x − 4| = |x − 1| · |x + 4|. What is |x + 4| called?","a":"the leftover","w":["the displacement","the first promise","the ceiling"],"why":"Expose |x − z| as a factor; what is left is the leftover."},
 {"q":"Under |x − 1| < 1, |x + 4| < 6. What is 6 called?","a":"a ceiling on the leftover","w":["the first promise","the threshold","the reach"],"why":"Tame the leftover: the first promise gives it a ceiling B."},
 {"q":"First promise r, ceiling B on the leftover. What δ answers ε?","a":"min(r, ε/B)","w":["max(r, ε/B)","ε/B alone","r · ε/B"],"why":"Both promises at once: the least of the two."},
 {"q":"x² + 3x near 1: δ = ε/6 alone, with ε = 30. Does it win?","a":"no: x = 5.9 escapes","w":["yes: ε/6 is the recipe","yes: 30 is a large gap","no: ε/6 is not positive"],"why":"The ceiling 6 holds only inside the first promise |x − 1| < 1."},
 {"q":"1/x near ½: first promise |x − ½| < ¼. What is its second job?","a":"keep x away from 0","w":["keep x above ½","make ε smaller","make the leftover a constant"],"why":"Near 0 the leftover 2/|x| has no ceiling."},
 {"q":"|√x − 2| = |x − 4|/(√x + 2). Which move exposed the displacement?","a":"times the conjugate over itself","w":["factor and cancel","a first promise","divide by the greatest power"],"why":"Difference-of-squares on the top clears the root."},
 {"q":"A write-up opens: “Put δ = …”, then “Let ε > 0.” What is wrong?","a":"δ is named before ε","w":["nothing: either order","ε must be named last","“Put” is not a move"],"why":"δ may hang on ε, so ε comes first: speak of everything."},
 {"q":"A write-up supposes |x − w| < δ with no 0 <. What fails?","a":"a function with a stray value at w","w":["every linear function","nothing: 0 < is decoration","only x² near 0"],"why":"Without 0 <, the value at w is asked to keep the promise."},
 {"q":"¬(lim_{x→z} f(x) = L) pushed inward. What does it become?","a":"∃ε > 0 ∀δ > 0 ∃x (0 < |x − z| < δ ∧ ε ≤ |f(x) − L|)","w":["∀ε > 0 ∃δ > 0 ∀x (0 < |x − z| < δ ⇒ ε ≤ |f(x) − L|)","∃ε > 0 ∃δ > 0 ∃x (0 < |x − z| < δ ∧ ε ≤ |f(x) − L|)","∀ε > 0 ∀δ > 0 ∃x (0 < |x − z| < δ ∧ ε ≤ |f(x) − L|)"],"why":"Not-heading-to: each sign turns, and the if becomes an and."},
 {"q":"The challenger shows that for δ = 0.1 the input 2.05 escapes. Is the claim refuted?","a":"no: every δ must be beaten","w":["yes: one escape is enough","yes, if ε is ¼","no: 2.05 is not in the window"],"why":"One ε must beat every δ."},
 {"q":"A recipe for x² reads min(1, ε/9). Where is z?","a":"z = 4 or z = −4","w":["z = 9","z = 3 or z = −3","z = 1"],"why":"The ceiling is 1 + 2|z| = 9."}];
/* phase F und_r10: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["10a"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["10a"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"10a1.1","q":"What jobs do ε and δ have? What is ∀ε > 0 P(ε) short for?","a":"ε is the challenger's gap around L and δ is your reach around w; it is short for ∀ε (ε ∈ ℝ ∧ 0 < ε ⇒ P(ε))","w":["ε is your reach and δ is the challenger's gap; it is short for ∃ε (0 < ε ∧ P(ε))","ε and δ both name the limit; it is short for P(0)","ε is the gap and δ is the reach; it is short for ∀ε (ε ∈ ℝ ∧ 0 < ε ∧ P(ε))"],"why":"A bounded every unpacks with ⇒ and a bounded some with ∧. With ∧, the last line would claim every thing is a positive real."},
 {"u":"10a2.1","q":"What is the neighbourhood of w of reach δ, and why is w itself taken out? What does “f is defined near w” mean?","a":"{x : 0 < |x − w| < δ}; the claim is about w's neighbours, not w; some neighbourhood of w is a part of dom f","w":["{x : |x − w| ≤ δ}; w is taken out because f has no value there; f is defined at w","{x : 0 < |x − w| < δ}; w is taken out because it is 0; f is defined on all of ℝ","the interval (w, w + δ); the left side is never used; f is defined to the right of w"],"why":"It is (w − δ, w) ∪ (w, w + δ). f may have a value at w or not; the game never asks."},
 {"u":"10a3.1","q":"What does the line say, in words? Why must the signs stand in the order ∀ε ∃δ and not ∃δ ∀ε?","a":"however small a gap is named around L, some neighbourhood of w keeps f inside it; swapped, one reach would have to serve every gap","w":["f(w) = L; the order of the signs makes no difference","some gap around L holds f near w; swapped, it says the same","however small a reach is named, some gap holds; swapped, δ could be 0"],"why":"The reach answers the gap, so it comes after it. One δ for every ε claims f is exactly L on a neighbourhood: true for constants, false for 3x."},
 {"u":"10a4.1","q":"What does the agreeing-near technique say? What is a hole, and why is 9a’s hole harmless?","a":"functions equal on a neighbourhood of w have the same limits there; a hole is being defined near w but not at w; H agrees with x + 1 near 1","w":["functions equal at w have the same limits there; a hole is a limit that fails; H(1) = 2","functions with the same limit are equal near w; a hole is a value of 0; H is never used","functions equal on a neighbourhood have the same value at w; a hole is a stray value; H(1) = 3"],"why":"The 0 < keeps w out of the game. So lim_{x→1} (x² − 1)/(x − 1) = 2, with no value at 1 or with a stray one."},
 {"u":"10a5.1","q":"What does the line-limit technique say, and what is the trick its proof runs on?","a":"lim_{x→w} (mx + b) = mw + b; rewrite the output's distance until the input's distance appears inside it","w":["lim_{x→w} (mx + b) = b; put x = 0","lim_{x→w} (mx + b) = mw + b; put x = w and read the value","lim_{x→w} (mx + b) = m; divide the outputs by the inputs"],"why":"|(mx + b) − (mw + b)| = |m| · |x − w|. So δ = ε/|m| answers every gap when m ≠ 0."},
 {"u":"10a6.1","q":"Why is δ = ε/|x + w| an answer to check for x², and what does the first promise do instead?","a":"δ is named before any x, so a δ with x in it names nothing; promising |x − w| < 1 puts a ceiling on the leftover |x + w|","w":["the answer is sound, since x is known first; the first promise is spare","|x + w| may be 0; the first promise makes x positive","δ must be a whole number; the first promise rounds it"],"why":"The line says ∃δ ∀x. At w = 2 the promise gives |x + 2| < 5, so δ = min(1, ε/5) wins every round."},
 {"u":"10a7.1","q":"In the proof of one-over-x-limit, what second job does the first promise do, and how is its size chosen?","a":"it keeps the inputs away from 0; it is half the distance from w to 0, so |x| > |w|/2","w":["it keeps the inputs away from w; it is 1, as for x²","it makes the inputs positive; it is twice |w|","it keeps the outputs below 1; it is ε"],"why":"Near 0 the leftover 1/(|x||w|) has no ceiling. With |x − w| < |w|/2 it is below 2/w², and δ = min(|w|/2, w²ε/2) wins."},
 {"u":"10a8.1","q":"Why is δ = 0 an answer to check? Why do two won rounds not prove the limit?","a":"no x has 0 < |x − w| < 0, so every L would win by free-if; the line says every ε, and two rounds prove two rounds","w":["δ = 0 is the best reach, since it is the smallest; two rounds are enough when ε is small","δ = 0 makes x = w; a third round always settles it","δ may be 0 when f is constant; two rounds prove it for all smaller gaps"],"why":"δ must be positive. And an every is proved by letting ε be anything positive, not by samples."},
 {"u":"10a9.1","q":"Handed a recipe δ = ε/7 that always wins for a linear function, how is the step recovered? Handed a function with no value at w, how is its limit found?","a":"the line needs |m| · ε/7 ≤ ε, so |m| ≤ 7; find a function that agrees with it near w and cite agreeing-near","w":["m = 7 exactly; give the function the value 0 at w","|m| ≥ 7; it has no limit","the line needs ε/7 ≤ δ; put x = w"],"why":"Run backwards, a recipe tells what it was for. A hole does not stop a limit, because the value at the point plays no part."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["10b"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["10b"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"10b1.1","q":"What are the two halves that “the limit of f at w” must earn, and which one can fail?","a":"a some and an at-most-one; the some can fail, since a function may have no limit at all","w":["a some and an at-most-one; the at-most-one can fail","an every and a some; neither can fail","a value at w and a neighbourhood; the value can fail"],"why":"∃! joins the two. The at-most-one is what this step proves, and it licenses computing with lim f(x) as a real."},
 {"u":"10b2.1","q":"What does the two-targets technique say, and why is half the distance the right gap?","a":"if 2ε ≤ |L − M|, no real is within ε of both L and M; the triangle inequality would give |L − M| < 2ε","w":["if ε ≤ |L − M|, no real is within ε of both; any gap under the distance works","some real is within ε of both L and M for every ε","if 2ε ≤ |L − M|, then L = M"],"why":"|L − M| ≤ |L − y| + |y − M| < ε + ε. With a wider gap the targets overlap: 2.5 is within 3 of both 1 and 4."},
 {"u":"10b3.1","q":"What is the plan of the one-limit proof? Where does the either-way principle enter it?","a":"assume L ≠ M, challenge both claims with half the distance, take one input in both windows, and clash with two-targets; through not-not-out","w":["compute both limits and compare; it never enters","assume L = M and derive it again; in choosing ε","challenge with ε = |L − M| and take x = w; in the triangle inequality"],"why":"Refuting L ≠ M gives ¬¬(L = M). Taking the nots out to reach L = M is proof by contradiction proper."},
 {"u":"10b4.1","q":"What does limits-keep-order say, and what does it not say? What does sign-kept-near say?","a":"f ≤ g near w gives L ≤ M; strict order is not kept; a limit L > 0 makes f(x) > L/2 > 0 near w","w":["f < g near w gives L < M; nothing is left out; a positive limit makes f positive everywhere","f ≤ g near w gives L = M; order is lost; a limit of 0 makes f zero near w","L ≤ M gives f ≤ g near w; strict order is kept; f(w) > 0"],"why":"−|x| < |x| at every x ≠ 0, yet both head to 0 at 0. The order of outputs passes to the limits only as ≤."},
 {"u":"10b5.1","q":"How does limits-keep-order give one-limit a second time? Why does neither road avoid the either-way principle?","a":"f ≤ f near w gives L ≤ M and M ≤ L, so L = M by antisymmetry; keep-order closes with three-way-order on ℝ, which rests on it","w":["it gives L < M and M < L, a clash; the second road needs no principle","it takes ε = |L − M|; the triangle inequality is the principle","f = f gives L = M at once; no order is used"],"why":"The two roads share their engine: three-way-order on the line rests on 8b's comparison, and so on rung 7's either-way principle."},
 {"u":"10b6.1","q":"Why is ε = |L − M| a challenge to check? Why may the input not be w itself?","a":"the targets then overlap, so there is no clash; w is in no neighbourhood of w, and f may have no value there","w":["the gap is too small to answer; w is always the best input","ε must be 1; w is negative","the targets then never meet any output; f(w) is always L"],"why":"With L = 1 and M = 4, 2.5 is within 3 of both. And the promise 0 < |x − w| < δ says nothing at x = w."},
 {"u":"10b7.1","q":"Handed two claimed limits, how does the refutation open, and what does its last line need? Handed an inequality between outputs near w, what passes to the limits?","a":"with any ε up to half their distance; one input in both windows; the inequality passes as ≤","w":["with ε equal to their distance; the input w; it passes as <","with δ = 0; no input at all; nothing passes","with any ε up to half their distance; an input in one window; it passes as ="],"why":"Two-targets needs an output near both values at once, so take the least of the reaches. Limits-keep-order hands on ≤ only."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["10c"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["10c"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"10c1.1","q":"What functions do f + g, fg and f/g name, and on which inputs? What is min(p, q, r) short for?","a":"outputs added, multiplied, divided, on dom f ∩ dom g, and for f/g only where g(x) ≠ 0; min(min(p, q), r)","w":["f after g in each case, on dom g; the mean of p, q, r","outputs added, multiplied, divided, on dom f ∪ dom g; min(p, q) + r","outputs added, multiplied, divided, on dom f ∩ dom g with no further condition; the middle of the three"],"why":"A sum needs both outputs, so both inputs. The least of several reaches is at most each of them, and positive when they all are."},
 {"u":"10c2.1","q":"What does the limits-add technique say? Why does the proof give each function half of ε?","a":"lim (f + g) = L + M; the triangle inequality adds the two distances, and ε/2 + ε/2 = ε","w":["lim (f + g) = L · M; half is a custom","lim (f + g) = L + M; each function can only answer gaps below ε/2","lim (f + g) = (L + M)/2; the halves average"],"why":"|f(x) + g(x) − (L + M)| ≤ |f(x) − L| + |g(x) − M|. Each answers its budget with a reach, and you answer with the least."},
 {"u":"10c3.1","q":"In limits-multiply, why is f(x)M added and taken away? What does bounded-near supply?","a":"to make small pieces: f(x)(g(x) − M) + M(f(x) − L); a reach on which |f(x)| < |L| + 1","w":["to cancel LM; a reach on which f(x) = L","to make f(x) larger; a ceiling on |g(x) − M|","to make small pieces; a reach on which |f(x)| < ε"],"why":"f(x)g(x) − LM has no visible small piece. The factor |f(x)| is not small, so a first promise tames it before the budgets are set."},
 {"u":"10c4.1","q":"What does away-from-zero say, and what is it for? Why does limits-divide need M ≠ 0?","a":"if lim g = M ≠ 0, then |g(x)| > |M|/2 near w, so 1/g is defined there; with M = 0 there is no 1/M to head to","w":["if lim g = M, then g(x) = M near w; with M = 0 the quotient is 0","if lim g = 0, then g(x) ≠ 0 near w; M ≠ 0 is a custom","if lim g = M ≠ 0, then |g(x)| < |M|/2 near w; with M = 0 the top must be 0 too"],"why":"Challenge with ε = |M|/2. It also puts a ceiling on 1/|g(x)|, the first promise the proof for 1/g needs."},
 {"u":"10c5.1","q":"What does the substitution technique say, and what is its condition? How is power-limit proved?","a":"a polynomial heads to p(w), and p/q to p(w)/q(w) when q(w) ≠ 0; by induction on n with limits-multiply","w":["every function heads to its value; by trying n = 1, 2, 3","a polynomial heads to p(w), and p/q to p(w)/q(w) always; by limits-add","p/q heads to p(w)/q(w) when p(w) ≠ 0; by line-limit"],"why":"f⁰ is the constant 1, and fⁿ · f heads to Lⁿ · L. Each term of p then follows by constant-factor, and the sum by limits-add."},
 {"u":"10c6.1","q":"Why is giving f and g each the whole ε a move to watch out for? Why does ε/(2|M|) fail as a budget?","a":"the sum is then only within 2ε; at M = 0 the budget divides by 0","w":["the sum is then within ε/2; the budget is too large","the sum then has no limit; |M| may be negative","it is sound for sums; the budget fails only when M = 1"],"why":"Split the gap so the pieces add back to ε, and use a bottom that cannot be 0."},
 {"u":"10c7.1","q":"Handed a recipe such as min(1, ε/8, ε/4), how is it read? Handed a combination whose pieces have no limits, what do the techniques say?","a":"a first promise of 1 and two budgets, each half of ε over the size of the other factor; they say nothing","w":["three gaps to try in turn; they say the combination has no limit","the least of three limits; they say the limit is 0","a first promise of 1 and two budgets; they say the limit is the sum of the pieces"],"why":"The techniques are ifs: with a condition unmet they are silent. Then you play the game, or go round by 10d."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["10d"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["10d"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"10d1.1","q":"What does the squeeze technique say? What does its column never use?","a":"if g ≤ f ≤ h near w and g and h both head to L, then f heads to L; it never uses any value of f","w":["if g ≤ f near w and g heads to L, then f heads to L; it never uses g","if g ≤ f ≤ h near w, then f has a limit; it never uses the reach","if g ≤ f ≤ h near w and g and h both head to L, then f = L near w; it never uses h"],"why":"L − ε < g(x) ≤ f(x) ≤ h(x) < L + ε. The column knows f only through the trap, so it works on functions with no formula."},
 {"u":"10d2.1","q":"What do squeezed-to-0 and squeezed-by-distance say?","a":"|f(x)| ≤ h(x) near w with h heading to 0 gives f heading to 0; |f(x) − L| ≤ C · |x − w| near w gives f heading to L","w":["|f(x)| ≤ h(x) gives f = h; |f(x) − L| ≤ C gives f heading to L","f(x) ≤ h(x) with h heading to 0 gives f heading to 0; |f(x) − L| ≥ C · |x − w| gives f heading to L","|f(x)| ≥ h(x) gives f heading to 0; f(x) ≤ C · x gives f heading to C"],"why":"|f(x)| ≤ h(x) is −h(x) ≤ f(x) ≤ h(x), a trap from both sides. And |x − w| itself heads to 0 at w, with δ = ε."},
 {"u":"10d3.1","q":"What does the heads-nowhere technique say, and what is the plan of its proof? Why does x · q(x) head to 0 at 0?","a":"q heads to no L at any w: challenge with ½, and every window holds a fraction and an irrational, outputs 1 and 0; |x · q(x)| ≤ |x|","w":["q heads to ½ at every w: average the two outputs; x · q(x) = x","q heads to 0 at every irrational: challenge with 1; q(0) = 0","q has a limit only at fractions: take δ = 1; x is a fraction"],"why":"No L is within ½ of both 1 and 0. But a factor that heads to 0 tames the wild one: squeezed-to-0 with h(x) = |x|."},
 {"u":"10d4.1","q":"What does this footing say the squeeze was before it was about functions?","a":"a trap for numbers: 1.4142 < √2 < 1.4143, and the cut-offs c_k ≤ x < c_k + 10^(−k) for every k","w":["a way to draw graphs","a technique for adding decimals","a test for whether a number is a fraction"],"why":"8f trapped √2 between decimals and 8·cb5 made the trap endless. The same trap now holds a whole function near an input."},
 {"u":"10d5.1","q":"What are the three things this footing says a squeeze cannot do without?","a":"traps that head to the same value, in place on a whole neighbourhood, and on both sides","w":["traps with formulas, a value at w, and a drawing","one trap, a few inputs, and a constant","traps that are polynomials, equal at w, and positive"],"why":"0 ≤ q(x) ≤ 1 traps q between 0 and 1, and q has no limit. A trap at some inputs, or from one side, leaves the rest free."},
 {"u":"10d6.1","q":"What is the habit this footing teaches for a wild function? What does squeeze-for-sides say?","a":"split off what the techniques can do and squeeze only the rest; the squeeze holds on a half-window for a one-sided limit","w":["squeeze the whole function at once; the squeeze needs both sides always","rewrite the wild part; a trap on one side proves the two-sided limit","drop the wild part; the squeeze fails on half-windows"],"why":"x · q(x) + 3x + 2 at 0: the squeeze gives 0 for the wild part, line-limit gives 2, and limits-add puts them together."},
 {"u":"10d7.1","q":"Why is a trap at the fractions only not a squeeze? Why does x · q(x) → 0 not show that q → 0?","a":"the hypothesis is for every x near w, and inputs left out are free; the factor x did the work","w":["fractions are too few to matter; q is 0 near 0","a trap must be at the irrationals; q → 0 does follow","the fractions have no limits; x · q(x) has no limit either"],"why":"Without the factor, heads-nowhere says q has no limit at all."},
 {"u":"10d8.1","q":"Wanting lim f = L with no rewrite in sight, what do you write f(x) − L as, and which technique closes? Wanting to refute a limit for a wild function, what do you hunt for?","a":"a product of a factor you can bound and a factor heading to 0, closed by squeezed-to-0; two kinds of input in every window with outputs a fixed distance apart","w":["a sum of two limits, closed by limits-add; one input where f is large","a quotient, closed by limits-divide; a window where f is constant","a product of two bounded factors, closed by the squeeze; the value of f at w"],"why":"A bounded factor times one that shrinks is trapped by the shrinking one. Two outputs a fixed distance apart cannot both sit in a small target."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["10e"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["10e"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"10e1.1","q":"What does lim_{x→w⁺} f(x) = L say, and what must be true of f for it to be asked? What is the small high + in w⁺?","a":"the same game with only the inputs in w < x < w + δ asked; f must be defined just right of w; a part of the mark, naming no real","w":["the limit with L made larger; f must be positive; a power of w","the same game with inputs in w − δ < x < w; f must be defined at w; the next real after w","the limit of f(x) + w; f must be defined on ℝ; a sign of a number"],"why":"Each half of the neighbourhood gets its own game. The + says only that the inputs come from the greater side."},
 {"u":"10e2.1","q":"What does two-sides-agree say? Which direction is free, and which needs the least of two reaches?","a":"the limit at w is L exactly when both one-sided limits are L; whole to halves is free, halves to whole takes min(δ⁻, δ⁺)","w":["the limit is the mean of the two sides; both directions are free","the limit is L when one side is L; the right side needs the greater reach","the two sides always agree; neither direction needs a reach"],"why":"A promise for the whole neighbourhood is a promise for each half. Going back, the answer must keep both sides' promises."},
 {"u":"10e3.1","q":"Why does |x|/x have no limit at 0? When do two formulas glued at a point have a limit there?","a":"it heads to 1 from the right and −1 from the left; exactly when the two formulas head to the same value","w":["it has no value at 0; exactly when the value at the point matches","it heads to 0 from both sides; never","it passes every bound; when one of the formulas is constant"],"why":"Two-sides-differ. The value at the point is never consulted: the glued f heads to 1 at 1 though f(1) = 5."},
 {"u":"10e4.1","q":"Why does 10a’s line not apply to √x at 0, and what does root-at-0 say? What does no-side-at-0 say?","a":"√x has no input left of 0, so it is not defined near 0; lim_{x→0⁺} √x = 0 with δ = ε²; 1/x has no limit at 0 from either side","w":["√0 names nothing; lim_{x→0} √x = 0 with δ = ε; 1/x heads to 0 at 0","√x is negative left of 0; lim_{x→0⁻} √x = 0; 1/x has a limit from the right only","√x is too steep; lim_{x→0⁺} √x = 0 with δ = 2ε; 1/x heads to 1"],"why":"For 0 < x < ε², √x < ε. And whatever L is claimed for 1/x, an input small enough gives an output past |L| + 1."},
 {"u":"10e5.1","q":"What does the sides-too technique say, and why do the columns carry over? What is a jump, and what is its size?","a":"the limit techniques hold with x → w⁺ or x → w⁻; their columns only take an input in the window and the least of the reaches; one-sided limits that disagree, |L⁺ − L⁻|","w":["only limits-add holds on one side; the others use both halves; a hole, of size 0","the techniques hold on one side with twice the reach; a jump is a value at w, of size f(w)","none of the techniques hold on one side; a jump is where f has no inputs"],"why":"Both moves work on a half-window, and nothing else changes. For |x|/x at 0 the jump is 2."},
 {"u":"10e6.1","q":"Why is lim_{x→0} √x = 0 a line to check? Why is δ = max(δ⁻, δ⁺) an answer to check?","a":"√x is not defined near 0, so only the right-hand line applies; the larger reach breaks the other side's promise","w":["√0 is not 0; the larger reach is always safe","the limit is 1; the reaches must be equal","√x has two values; max is not defined for reals"],"why":"With no input on the left, the two-sided line is not asked. And the answer must keep both promises, so take the least."},
 {"u":"10e7.1","q":"Handed a function in pieces, or with an absolute value in it, how is its limit found? Handed a claimed limit, what refutes it?","a":"find where the formula changes and play each side with that side's formula; one side that disagrees or has no limit","w":["substitute the point into either formula; a value at the point that differs","use the left formula only; a side that agrees","average the two formulas; a larger δ"],"why":"Two-sides-agree runs both ways: the limit needs both sides to head to the one value."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["10f"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["10f"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"10f1.1","q":"What are the two licences every move in this step rests on? What are the three things that can happen when you substitute into a fraction?","a":"the limit techniques and agreeing-near; the bottom is not 0 and the value is the limit, only the bottom is 0, or both are 0, a signal","w":["drawing and measuring; the answer is 0, 1 or ∞","the squeeze and one-limit; the fraction is positive, negative or 0","the limit techniques and agreeing-near; the top is 0 and there is no limit, or the bottom is 0 and the limit is 0, or both are 0 and it is 1"],"why":"Substitute first, always. Both 0 means the factor x − w hides in top and bottom: find it, cancel it, and substitute again."},
 {"u":"10f2.1","q":"Why may x − 1 be cancelled in (x² − 1)/(x − 1) though it is 0 at 1? What finds the factor when it is not visible?","a":"the two functions agree at every x ≠ 1, and agreeing-near needs no more; factor says it is there and polynomial-division finds the rest","w":["0/0 = 1; guessing finds it","the limit is taken at x = 1, where both are 2; the conjugate finds it","cancelling is always sound; substitution finds it"],"why":"The functions are not the same: one has a hole at 1. Their limits are the same, since the value at the point plays no part."},
 {"u":"10f3.1","q":"What does root-limit say, and which 10d technique proves it? What does multiplying by the conjugate do to a 0/0 with a root in it?","a":"lim_{x→w} √(x + c) = √(w + c) when w + c > 0, by squeezed-by-distance; it clears the root so the hidden factor shows","w":["lim √(x + c) = √w + √c, by the squeeze; it makes the bottom 0","lim √(x + c) = w + c, by heads-nowhere; it doubles the root","lim √(x + c) = √(w + c) for every w and c, by limits-multiply; it changes the limit's value"],"why":"|√(x + c) − √(w + c)| ≤ |x − w|/√(w + c). And multiplying by P/P is multiplying by 1 where P ≠ 0."},
 {"u":"10f4.1","q":"When does the squeeze do the work in this step, and why could no rewrite have found the limit of (x − 1)² · q(x) at 1?","a":"when there is no algebra to grab: bound |f(x) − L| by something heading to 0; q has no formula to rewrite","w":["when the fraction is 0/0; q cancels","always, before substituting; q is a polynomial","when the bottom is 0; q is 0 near 1"],"why":"|(x − 1)² q(x)| ≤ (x − 1)², which heads to 0 at 1. The traps meet, so squeezed-to-0 applies."},
 {"u":"10f5.1","q":"What does lim_{x→∞} f(x) = L say, and what takes the place of the reach? What does “f(x) passes every bound” say, and why is it not a limit?","a":"for every gap, past some threshold N the outputs stay within it; for every M the outputs get above M and stay; no L is claimed","w":["f(∞) = L, with δ = ∞; f(x) = ∞, which is the limit","for some gap, the outputs stay within it; f is large somewhere; its limit is the bound","for every gap, past some threshold the outputs stay within it; f rises at every input; its limit is ∞"],"why":"There is no point to be near, so you answer with a threshold. ∞ is not a number, and the ladder never writes a limit equal to it."},
 {"u":"10f6.1","q":"What does lim_{n→∞} u_n = L say for a sequence? What does the cut-offs technique say?","a":"∀ε > 0 ∃N ∈ ℕ: every term from the N-th on is within ε of L; the cut-offs of a decimal head to its real","w":["some term equals L; the cut-offs end","∃N ∀ε: the terms from N on equal L; the cut-offs head to 0","the terms grow past every bound; the cut-offs are all fractions, so their limit is a fraction"],"why":"|c_k − x| < 10^(−k) < 1/k. So 1, 1.4, 1.41, 1.414, ⋯ heads to √2, though no term is √2."},
 {"u":"10f7.1","q":"Why is a limit of an absolute value split by sides, and how is each side computed?","a":"the bars are two formulas glued where the inside is 0; on each side use that side's formula, then compare","w":["the bars have no limit; each side is 0","the bars are always positive; drop them on both sides","the bars double the function; halve each side"],"why":"|x² − 1|/(x − 1) heads to 2 from the right of 1 and −2 from the left, so it has no limit at 1."},
 {"u":"10f8.1","q":"Why is 0/0 = 1 a move to watch out for? Why is cancelling the x’s in (x² + 4)/(x + 2) a move to watch out for?","a":"0/0 names nothing and is only a signal: such limits come out as anything; cancel factors, not terms","w":["0/0 = 0; the x's cancel to leave 4/2","0/0 is ∞; x + 2 is a factor of x² + 4","0/0 = 1 is sound for limits; terms cancel when the powers differ"],"why":"The 0/0 limits of this step came out 2, 5/4, 12, ¼, −1/9. And at −2 the top x² + 4 is 8, so x + 2 is not a factor of it."},
 {"u":"10f9.1","q":"How is a 0/0 limit built from its answer?","a":"start from a simplified form with the wanted value at w, hide the factor x − w in top and bottom, and expand","w":["start from 0/0 and choose any answer","put w in the top and 0 in the bottom","multiply the answer by 0 on both sides"],"why":"((x + 3)(x − 4))/(x − 4) = (x² − x − 12)/(x − 4) is 0 over 0 at 4 and heads to 7. Factor and cancel undoes the build."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["10g"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["10g"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"10g1.1","q":"What are the three beats of the scratch work? Which direction is the proof?","a":"expose the displacement, tame the leftover, read off δ; the forward one, from “let ε > 0” to “< ε”","w":["guess δ, test it, guess again; the backward one","substitute, factor, cancel; either direction","name x, name δ, name ε; the backward one"],"why":"The scratch work runs back from the goal and finds δ. Only the forward writing, with every line licensed, is the proof."},
 {"u":"10g2.1","q":"For a linear function, what is the leftover, and what does the answer undo?","a":"the constant |m|; δ = ε/|m| undoes the stretch of distances by |m|","w":["x itself; the answer undoes the shift b","0; nothing needs undoing","|x − w|; the answer undoes the square"],"why":"|f(x) − L| = |m| · |x − w|. With a constant leftover there is nothing to tame."},
 {"u":"10g3.1","q":"Why must δ for x² + 3x at 1 keep both promises, min(1, ε/6)?","a":"the ceiling 6 on |x + 4| was earned only inside |x − 1| < 1, so ε/6 alone can lose for big gaps","w":["ε/6 is too small for small gaps","1 is the limit","δ must be a whole number"],"why":"With ε = 30 the answer ε/6 = 5 lets in x = 5.9, where |x + 4| is 9.9 and the output escapes."},
 {"u":"10g4.1","q":"For 1/x near ½, what sizes the first promise, and why?","a":"half the distance from ½ to 0, so ¼: it keeps x > ¼ and puts the ceiling 8 on 2/|x|","w":["1, as for x²: any promise will do","the whole distance ½: it lets x reach 0","ε: the promise must shrink with the gap"],"why":"A promise of 1 would let the window reach 0, where the leftover has no ceiling. So δ = min(¼, ε/8)."},
 {"u":"10g5.1","q":"In the root’s scratch work, what exposes the displacement, and what is the first promise for?","a":"times the conjugate over itself; the 4 in min(4, 2ε) keeps the window inside the domain [0, ∞)","w":["squaring both sides; the promise bounds the leftover by 4","substitution; the promise makes x a square","times the conjugate over itself; the promise bounds the leftover by 2ε"],"why":"|√x − 2| = |x − 4|/(√x + 2) ≤ |x − 4|/2 with no promise needed for the ceiling. A first promise can do either job."},
 {"u":"10g6.1","q":"How is a recipe for a sum built from the recipes of its pieces?","a":"give each piece half the gap and take the lesser of their answers","w":["give each piece the whole gap and take the greater answer","add the two answers","multiply the two answers"],"why":"For x² + 3x near 1: x² answers ε/2 with min(1, ε/6) and 3x with ε/6. The lesser is min(1, ε/6), what the three beats found directly."},
 {"u":"10g7.1","q":"What is on the checklist for a write-up?","a":"open with “let ε > 0”, name δ before any x, suppose 0 < |x − w| < δ, spend each promise where it is needed, end with |f(x) − L| < ε","w":["open with x, then name ε, then δ, and end with a drawing","name δ last, after the algebra is done","open with “let δ > 0”, name ε to fit, and end with |x − w| < δ"],"why":"The order of the written lines is the order of the moves, and a line that uses a bound needs the line above to have earned it."},
 {"u":"10g8.1","q":"What does the not-heading-to technique say, read as a strategy? Which direction of it needs rung 7?","a":"the challenger names one gap and beats every reach with an input whose output escapes; from a bare denial to the strategy","w":["the challenger beats one reach with every gap; neither direction","the challenger names a new gap for each reach; from the strategy to the denial","you name one reach that fails; both directions"],"why":"¬(lim f = L) ⇔ ∃ε ∀δ ∃x (0 < |x − w| < δ ∧ ε ≤ |f(x) − L|). Strategy to denial is rung 1's counterexample, not-some and negated-if."},
 {"u":"10g9.1","q":"Why is beating one δ not a refutation? Why is dropping the 0 < in a write-up a danger?","a":"the challenger must beat every δ with one ε; without 0 < the value at w counts, fatal for a function with a stray value there","w":["one δ is enough if it is small; the 0 < is only style","the challenger must beat one δ with every ε; without 0 < δ may be 0","a refutation needs two δ's; without 0 < x may be negative"],"why":"The denial reads ∃ε ∀δ ∃x. And the limit ignores the point itself: harmless for x², not for a hole with a stray value."},
 {"u":"10g10.1","q":"Handed the recipe min(1, ε/9) for x², how is w recovered? Handed a claim you think false, how is the gap chosen?","a":"the ceiling 9 = 1 + 2|w| gives w = ±4; find the true value and name a gap less than the distance to it","w":["w = 9; name the gap 1","the ceiling 9 = w² gives w = ±3; name any gap","w = ε/9; name a gap greater than the distance to the true value"],"why":"A first promise of 1 puts |x + w| below 1 + 2|w|. And a target that misses the true value cannot hold the outputs."});
